>The whole GAME is arbitrary and inexplicable.

Au contraire.
Mummy wrappings blocking invisibility is a The Invisible Man reference.
Potions of sickness are biologically contaminated fruit juice. Sinks
cancelling levitation is a pun, as is amethyst into booze. Eating
amulets of unchanging un-changes you. Pyrolisk gazes require eye
contact from both participants. Elves don't respect Elbereth because
Her Name only protects you from "monsters". Facing is too hard to
implement with ASCII characters. Magic cancellation is just recognising
that some monsters' attacks require skin contact for their effects to
work. Knights can only jump in chess moves because Knights obey the
rules. Rust monsters are not made of rust - check Wikipedia. Mariliths
can't wield six weapons because then they'd slice their arms off. There
can only be one monster per square because you can't overlay ASCII
characters. You can't have elf healers because all healers must be
neutral. The Platinum Yendorian Express card "charges" items. Guides
can cast spells because they're supposed to be Rincewind. Bat corpses
make you batty. Reflection does not mean "reflective." The Cyclops is
the healer quest nemesis because Apollo killed him in revenge for
Aesculapius's death. Scorpius's castle is reused from the Elf quest.
The Romans had plumbling, so sinks aren't quite that contradictory.
David's Treasure Zoo is named after the guy who coded it. Twoflower
wore a Hawaiian shirt in The Colour of Magic. There are no non-silver
sabers because the silver saber is an anachronism, included solely as a
reference to Zelazny's Amber. Your backpack isn't an item because it'd
break the game - and not in your favour. You can eat K rations in one
move for balance purposes. Athames are specially built for engraving so
they don't dull. Gargoyles are _not_ made of rock (but they can resist
stoning.) Elves are chaotic because D&D said so - no more, no less.
Orcs can be wizards because orc-shamans are in the game. Lawful doesn't
equal Good, and Good doesn't equal Holy. Marduk is the superset
containing all of the aligned deities. See previous sentence for reason
why you can buy protection from cross-aligned priests. Lizards cure
stoning because Andries Brouwer had a reason which he can't remember
now (I asked.) The main ingredient in black puddings is blood.
Salamanders are bipedal humanoids, and not axolotls. Lifesavingless
conduct is already in the game - if you fulfill it, it says "You
survived" without adding "after being killed." Speed + Fruit juice =
Booze is a representaiton of the brewing process. Crystal balls are
considered "heavy" because they are cumbersome.  Mummies are sometimes
just well-preserved zombies - hence some have no mummy wrappings.
Monsters only sometimes leave corpses because your attacks have mangled
it into pieces. Master Kaen can sometimes be generated female, and
since you don't know the first name, this is acceptable. There are no
empty potion bottles because of balance. The sticks to snakes spell is
based on a passage in the Old Testament. Shopkeepers know your name
because there is no reason to lie to them. You can't move diagonally in
open doors because the open door is in the way. Monsters can't genocide
your role because nothing in the dungeon is permitted to genocide any
role besides their own. Daggers and knives have different skill slots
because only one can be used for outright stabbing. Chaotic gods use
demons as minions because they don't care if they're unholy. Weaponless
isn't expected of a monk, but armorless and vegetarian are. And
finally, _everything_ is either for balance, respect for some fantasy
mythos, or because someone in the 12th century with access to ink and
parchment believed it was real.

There, see? The game's _completely_ and _utterly_ understandable.

